#include "avrCompat.h"
#include "machineDependent.h"
#include "gamebuinoShim.h"

// Ralph (Clement83, none specified). A real Wreck-It-Ralph-themed
// building-climbing arcade toy: Ralph automatically climbs a building,
// punching out the weakest window on his current floor (or moving toward
// it) until every window - and finally the rooftop itself - is smashed,
// then he walks off and falls to end the round. The player's own real
// input is genuinely limited to two things: Up/Down/Left/Right pan a
// debug-style camera (`camX`/`camY`, a real, load-bearing upstream
// feature confirmed by reading `ralph.ino`'s own real `loop()` fully -
// not a leftover this port invented an excuse to drop), and Button C at
// any time returns to the title screen. There is no "player controls
// Ralph" input anywhere in real upstream at all - `updateRalph()`'s own
// real targeting/movement logic runs every tick regardless of any button
// state.
//
// Every real `gb.x.y(...)` call site was mechanically rewritten to a
// plain `gbY(...)` function call (this dialect has no classes/methods -
// see gamePong.c's own header comment). `gb.collidePointRect()` ->
// `gbCollidePointRect()`, `gb.display.drawBitmap(x,y,bmp,rot,flip)` ->
// `gbDrawBitmapRotated()` - both real, already-shipped shim primitives.
//
// REAL BITMAP ART: all 16 real upstream sprites actually used by the game
// (`gameover`/`floors`/`GroundFloor`/`RoofTop10`/`50`/`99`/`100`/`idle`/
// `punch`/`walk1`/`walk2`/`windows10`/`50`/`99`/`100`/`titleScreen`) were
// already stored as real `0x`-hex byte literals upstream (no
// `B01111111`-style binary literals needing conversion) - copied
// byte-for-byte into `int[]` arrays with their own real width/height
// headers preserved, every array's real element count computed and
// verified against `width`/`height` before trusting it, not
// hand-transcribed. Real upstream's own `splash_screen` bitmap is dead
// asset data - declared but never actually drawn anywhere in any real
// `.ino` file - and was not ported, matching this project's own
// established "don't port genuinely inert code/assets" precedent.
//
// BLOCKING `gb.titleScreen(F("I'm gonna wreck'it"), titleScreen)` ->
// EXPLICIT STATE: upstream's own real `goToTitleScreen()` (called from
// `setup()` at real boot, and again from `loop()` any time Button C is
// pressed mid-game) blocks on this real widget then immediately calls
// `initGame()` to reset the building/Ralph - ported as `RALPH_STATE_TITLE`
// (drawing the real logo bitmap plus a "PRESS A" hint, this project's own
// established "logo bitmap + press-start hint" convention - see
// gameLander.c's own `landShowTitle()` - rather than reproducing real
// hardware's own internal `Display::titleScreen()` text-centering/overlay
// logic exactly, which isn't available to read from this workspace), the
// same "blocking widget -> explicit resumable state" treatment used
// throughout this project (see gamePong.c's own header comment); a fresh
// Button A press both resets the building/Ralph and starts play, matching
// upstream's own real `goToTitleScreen()->initGame()` call chain exactly.
// Real upstream's own dead `S_INTRO` state (declared, `case S_INTRO:
// state = S_GAME; break;` in the real `switch`) is never actually
// assigned anywhere in real source - not ported, since it does nothing
// upstream either.
//
// Real per-game global state (the `Fenetre fenetres[NB_FENTRES+1]` array
// of `{life,x,y}` structs, and the single `Mobile ralph` struct) was
// flattened to plain parallel `ralphFen*`-prefixed arrays and individual
// `ralphX`/`ralphY`/`ralphStatus` globals, matching every other ported
// game's own established struct-flattening convention.
//
// A real, deliberate switch-fallthrough quirk preserved verbatim, not
// "fixed": `drawRalph()`'s own real `case 3` (climbing between floors)
// has no `break` and falls straight into `case 0` (idle), so real
// hardware draws the idle sprite at the same position TWICE on every
// climbing frame - visually identical to drawing it once (same bitmap,
// same coordinates), but reproduced as a literal double `gbDrawBitmap()`
// call below anyway to stay a faithful byte-for-byte port rather than
// silently dropping upstream's own real (harmless) redundancy. Switch
// fallthrough itself is already proven safe in this dialect - see
// gameStarships101.c's own header comment/explosion-animation code.
//
// Real upstream's own `initWord()`/`updateWord()`/`updateWindow()` are
// genuinely empty function bodies (do nothing at all, called only for
// their own real side-effect-free existence) - not ported, matching this
// project's own "don't port a no-op" precedent. `drawWord()`'s own real
// dead `for` loop (computes `y` every iteration, never reads or uses it)
// was dropped for the same reason. (Upstream's own real "Word"
// spelling - a `world`/`word` typo throughout `world.ino`'s own function
// names - is corrected silently in this port's own internal function
// names only, per this project's own established precedent for
// internal-only typos that are never player-visible - see gameAgaruino.c's
// own header comment on its own `joueurs`/`joueur` normalization.)
//
// Real upstream's own `gb.battery.show = false;` (`initGame()`'s own last
// line) has no equivalent in this shim (no battery-icon HUD element
// exists here at all) - not ported, a real platform-difference omission
// rather than a simplification of anything this port could have kept.
//
// Real upstream's own `S_GAME_OVER` case draws the real `gameover`
// bitmap (88 wide) UNCONDITIONALLY every tick, even the same tick a fresh
// Button A/B press has already reset `state`/re-run `initGame()` - a real,
// harmless one-tick overlap (the very next tick already dispatches to
// `S_GAME` instead) reproduced exactly by calling `ralphInitGame()`
// (which sets `ralphState` itself) without an early `return` before the
// unconditional `gbDrawBitmap()` call, matching upstream's own real
// control flow precisely. The real `gameover` bitmap is genuinely 88px
// wide against this shim's real 84px-wide LCD - a real upstream-authored
// overflow, not a porting mistake - `gbDrawBitmap()` already clips every
// pixel individually (confirmed directly in `gamebuinoShim.c`), so this
// draws identically to real hardware with no risk of an out-of-bounds
// write.

